# Code Reduction & Simplification

Apply when the user asks to simplify, reduce, streamline, or minimize code.

## Priority 1: Line Count & DOM Depth

Minimize total lines and HTML nesting. Every edit should reduce or hold steady on both. Flatten wrapper `<div>`s; remove redundant elements before adding CSS or JS workarounds.

## Hard Constraints

- **No new abstractions** — Do not add CSS mixins/variables, BEM utility classes, or custom JavaScript unless explicitly requested.
- **No new partials** — Reuse existing Hugo partials. Do not create a new partial unless asked.
- **Off limits** — Do not modify `content/**/*.md`, `hugo.toml`, or `config.yaml`.

## HTML & CSS

- Keep markup strictly semantic (`<article>`, `<nav>`, `<time>`, etc.). Prefer fewer elements over styling wrappers.
- Prefer modern CSS over breakpoint sprawl: `clamp()`, `min()`, `max()`, `container queries`.
- Delete unused rules and duplicate selectors instead of adding overrides.

## Examples

```html
{{/* ❌ BAD — extra wrapper for layout */}}
<div class="blog-card">
  <div class="blog-card__inner">
    <h2>{{ .Title }}</h2>
  </div>
</div>

{{/* ✅ GOOD — semantic, flat */}}
<article>
  <h2>{{ .Title }}</h2>
</article>
```

```scss
// ❌ BAD — three breakpoints for one spacing scale
.title { font-size: 1.25rem; }
@media (min-width: 768px) { .title { font-size: 1.5rem; } }
@media (min-width: 1024px) { .title { font-size: 1.75rem; } }

// ✅ GOOD — one line, no media queries
.title { font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1rem + 1vw, 1.75rem); }
```

## After Every Edit

Report the net line count difference (e.g. `−12 lines`). If lines increased, explain why and offer a leaner alternative.
